interview- First, I will use the Sliding Window approach to solve this problem.

I will use two pointers, low and high, to represent the starting and ending positions of my window.

Initially, both pointers are at index 0, and the sum is 0.

I will expand the window by adding elements using the high pointer.

Whenever the current sum becomes greater than or equal to the target, I have found a valid subarray.

Now, I will calculate its length and update the minimum length.

After that, I will remove the element at the low pointer from the sum and move low forward. This helps me check whether I can get a smaller valid subarray.

I will continue this process until high reaches the end of the array.

If I never find a subarray whose sum is at least the target, I will return 0. Otherwise, I will return the minimum length.

class Solution {
  public:
    int smallestSubWithSum(int x, vector<int>& arr) {
        // code here
        int mini=INT_MAX;
        int i=0;
        int j=0;
        int sum=0;
        int n=arr.size();
        while(j<n){
            sum+=arr[j];
            while(sum>x){
                mini=min(mini,j-i+1);         // subarray length= j-i+1
                sum=sum-arr[i];
                i++;
            }
                j++;
        }
        if(mini==INT_MAX){
            return 0;
        }
        else{
            return mini;
